      {
        "id": 24,
        "code": "## Muster 3: Baumrekursion (Tree Recursion)\n\nBaumrekursion führt **mehrere** rekursive Aufrufe aus, um verschiedene Teile einer Struktur zu verarbeiten.\n\n### Beispiel: Baumsumme (Tree Sum)",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "id": 25,
        "code": "% tree_sum für Binärbäume definieren\n% Baumformat: [Wert, LinkerTeilbaum, RechterTeilbaum] oder []\n:- dynamic tree_sum/2.\n\n% Basisfall: Leerer Baum hat Summe 0\ntree_sum([], 0).\n\n% Rekursiver Fall: Summe = Wert + linke_Summe + rechte_Summe\ntree_sum([V, L, R], Sum) :-\n    tree_sum(L, LS),   % ← Erster rekursiver Aufruf\n    tree_sum(R, RS),   % ← Zweiter rekursiver Aufruf\n    Sum is V + LS + RS.",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "true.",
        "lastOutputHtml": "<pre class=\"text-result\" dir=\"ltr\">true.</pre>"
      },
      {
        "id": 26,
        "code": "### In Prolog testen",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "id": 27,
        "code": "% Test: tree_sum von [5, [3, [1, [], []], []], [2, [], []]]\n%       5\n%      / \\\n%     3   2\n%    /\n%   1\n\\+ \\+ (\n    tree_sum([5, [3, [1, [], []], []], [2, [], []]], _Sum),\n    format('Tree sum: ~w (expected 11)~n', [_Sum])\n).",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "Tree sum: 11 (expected 11)",
        "lastOutputHtml": "<pre class=\"text-result\" dir=\"ltr\">Tree sum: 11 (expected 11)</pre>"
      },
